  "articleSummary": "The article argues that self-hosting Docker makes the operator responsible for end-to-end security, whether running services at home or on public cloud VPSs, and emphasizes choosing and verifying protections appropriate to the environment. In a practical, tutorial-style guide, it covers deliberate image updates and version pinning with Docker Compose, Dependabot, and Renovate; secure secret handling with Docker Secrets, password managers, Vault, and AWS Secrets Manager; and defenses such as canary tokens, read-only volumes, firewalls, network segmentation, authenticated Nginx proxies, access controls, monitoring, and production checklists. It targets self-hosters and Docker users ranging from hobbyists to production operators, using an approachable, lightly humorous framing that describes attackers as “riff-raff” and presents the techniques as options to pick and choose.",
